moves.experience:
The three paper sections in a row (Strings, Text, In Use) each get a printed event instead of a plain edge. When a running head first enters:
- an IKB overprint of its rule rolls left to right over the ink rule (420ms), then dries back to ink;
- the index '4B—03' flaps digit by digit;
- the dot leaders run in behind the overprint;
- the count ('57 words', '05 plates', '04 stages') flaps once from a random figure to its value.

Closing folios get a quieter version (rule overprint only). Sound: a soft paper zip and two ticks. Rewind re-arms the heads, so the replay prints again.

**Contents**
The folio 'p. 04/06 In Use' becomes a button. Clicking it opens Contents underneath:
- six lines with dot leaders and page numbers;
- each section title set in its own instance, so the contents page is itself a ramp (Title 50/100 → Colophon 150/900);
- the current page in IKB with ▸.

Arrow keys move through the lines. Enter turns to the page with a page-turn sound, and the folio flaps through every number in between (01→02→03→04).

**Thumb index (desktop)**
The right edge carries a dictionary thumb index:
- six 3px tabs, each as tall as its section is long;
- the current tab in IKB;
- a hairline 'you are here' mark that rides scroll progress.

Hovering a tab slides out a 22px label tab ('04 In Use', Anybody 112/700).

moves.implementation:
- K2.secrets = { list: [{id, hint, label}], found: Set, unlock(id) }, persisted per viewer in localStorage. Wrap it in try/catch; the page works without it.
- unlock() runs flap() into the compartment, a Sound.ding variant, the toast, and aria-live text ('Found KERN, 5 of 12').
- K2.hellbox.add(n, source) is called by:
  - the stick;
  - the word swap;
  - stage C's loose-letter hook. Ask C to expose an event such as K2.emit('loose').
- Markup: <section class='jobcase' aria-labelledby> with CSS grid columns (2fr 1fr 1fr 2fr …). The hints are static, so the case reads without JS.
- Non-typing secrets:
  - 7 distinct hero strikes within 4s;
  - all 5 case plates opened;
  - the forged signature;
  - the Konami code.

- Add to Sound:
  - click(i, g): a Karplus–Strong buffer at playbackRate 2, 40ms;
  - zip();
  - turn(): a noise bandpass sweep from 3k to 800Hz, 180ms;
  - bell(): ding with a second partial at 2.76×;
  - lamp().
- All of them go through gate() and the 16-voice cap.
- Sound.pluck() sets Sound.meter[i] = 1.
- Chrome.tick: while any meter value is above .01, decay it with exp(−dt/.12), write 7 scaleY transforms, and report busy. Otherwise it is idle.
- Work's existing row pointerenter calls Sound.pluck(i+1, .25, pan, 1).

moves.experience:
The specimen shows its working, like a proof on a light table.

1. A Swiss 12-column grid on the real gutters (IKB columns at 6%, 1px edges) and an 8px baseline grid.
2. Section boxes with tabs, for example '§04 IN USE · 1404 × 1622'.
3. A live spec tag on every display element, for example 'ANYBODY 212/170 · W 132 · G 812 · TR −2%'. Tags on the hero letters, string words, board tiles and LOUD glyphs update as they move.
4. Margin dimension lines with end ticks ('40', '76').
5. Proofreader's marks in IKB with leader lines, used as editorial jokes:
   - the 4: 'tr? no — hairline by design';
   - 'move.': 'stet';
   - the riso card: '1px out of register — stet';
   - the threshold O: '~70× in §05';
   - the board: '2–5 random sorts before the target';
   - the footnotes: '‡ see p.06'.
6. Telemetry docked bottom-left: 'FRAME 16.4 ms · 61 fps · AWAKE 3/16: lenis, hero, pigment · PIGMENT webgl2 128/512 · DPR 1.5 · LIBS lenis ✓ matter ✓'. When you stop, you watch systems fall asleep: the single-rAF architecture, made visible for the developer jury.

Toggling flickers the overlay twice like a fluorescent tube, with a lamp click.

moves.implementation:
**Director system**
Active only when all of these hold:
- idle for more than 20s;
- env.fine is true and env.reduce is false;
- no case, menu, sheet, proof mode or stick is open.

**Scripts**
One per folio: an array of {t, at, act}. The anchor `at` is an element or a viewport fraction, resolved with one read at start. A Catmull-Rom path runs through the anchors, timed independently of frame rate.

**Actions use public APIs only**
- K2.hero.strike;
- K2.strings.pluck;
- K2.pigment.splat(..., {ghost: true}), so ghost strokes are never logged to the plate;
- Board.fidget(i);
- a new thin Work.preview(i) / Work.unpreview() that wraps the row pointerenter path.

**The ghost** is a DOM clone of .rig with its own transforms; the real Rig is untouched. It stops on markInput().

reject:
**GLOBAL AXIS MIXER over ALL type: reject as specified.** Every system owns its axes and its measure. A page-wide bias would:
- break the manifesto's line-lock (its lines are pre-broken at the final weight);
- overflow fitted lines, a horizontal-scroll risk at 320px;
- override the five client voices in the index;
- change glyph widths under live matter-js bodies.

Keep the idea where the engine already refits every frame: the title page. That means the + − < > keys, the LOUD and WHISPER presets and the scrubbable readouts. If a console is still wanted, make it a hero-only 'remix the title page' drawer.

moves.experience:
Clicking PROOF starts a 500ms plotter pass down the viewport that draws the page's real construction:
- the 12-column grid (4 on phone) with gutters tinted IKB 6%;
- the 8px baseline grid;
- the margins, with redline measurements ('↔ 40 · GUTTER');
- a box around every display word with cap and baseline, plus a live chip such as 'Anybody 150/900 · 212px · fit 1366'.

Then the systems open up:
- the hero chain shows its 7 nodes and spring coils with live displacement numbers;
- the strings show their control polygon and crossing node;
- the pigment draws its velocity field as an IKB hairline quiver inside the fluid;
- the LOUD letters show their matter-js hulls and home springs;
- the board shows its hinges;
- the threshold shows the zoom-origin crosshair and its ×scale.

A specimen-styled HUD sits bottom-left: FRAME 16.6ms · SIM 128×80 · DYE 512×320 · ACTIVE hero 0.4 / pigment 2.1 / rig 0.1 ms.

Everything stays fully interactive. Pressing G again retracts the lines upward. Aimed squarely at Developer-award jurors.

moves.implementation:
- Show system. Active when now − K2.lastInput > 16000, the document is visible, not reduced motion, the director is free and the folio is in SHOW_ACTS.
- Acts are keyframe arrays {t, fx, fy (fractions of the section rect), verb: move | pluck | strike | paint | hover | chip}, interpolated with Catmull-Rom.
- Verbs use public APIs only:
  - a synthetic Track fed into Hero, like Intro.ghost;
  - K2.hero.strike(i);
  - K2.strings.pluck;
  - K2.pigment.splat(…, {ghost:true});
  - Work.preview(i);
  - K2.loud.setWord.
- Synthetic actions must not call markInput.
- New Rig.drive(x, y, label).
- Sequencer: a lookahead scheduler (25ms tick, 100ms horizon) on ctx.currentTime. A 16-step pattern comes from mulberry32(plate number).
- A hard cap of 45s per idle period, then the page rests.

moves.experience:
A hard flick across a tape, or a click on the '+' crossing node, over-tensions it:
- it snaps at the crossing with a crack;
- the two halves whip away and hang from their anchors like dropped tape, with the words swinging and tilting on them;
- 2.4s later, tuning-peg ticks pull it back taut and it settles into its rest sag, while the folio caption flashes 'RE-STRUNG'.

It is the only moment in the first half where the visitor can break something.

A brayer (an ink roller with a highlight hairline) rolls down the viewport in 900ms, and behind it the specimen is reprinted on black stock:
- paper and ink swap; IKB stays;
- the threshold now zooms into a paper O, and the board becomes a day board;
- the pigment prints Monogold fields with IKB type, the most Klein state the page can reach.

Rolling again returns to day stock.
